	url = {https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/B9780443402944000323},
	doi = {10.1016/B978-0-443-40294-4.00032-3},
	abstract = {Solvent extraction is a unit operation applied in hydrometallurgical circuits to separate metals and constituents in aqueous solutions. When conditions are suitable, it can separate and concentrate the element or constituent of interest, transferring it into a purified aqueous solution. This chapter covers the basic concepts of solvent extraction. The starting aqueous solution is mixed with an immiscible organic solution containing an extractant reagent that can reversibly load and then release the target constituent depending on the properties of the aqueous phase. The parts of an extractant phase, extractant, diluent, and modifier are reviewed. Extraction mechanisms for the major types of extractant systems are explained. The most common types of equipment used in commercial solvent extraction circuits are reviewed, highlighting each type’s relative advantages and disadvantages. The major types of laboratory test work that may be performed to determine the design parameters and operating conditions for a commercial circuit are discussed. Finally, some commercial applications of solvent extraction are enumerated.},
	language = {en},
