	url = {http://www.jstor.org/stable/27796627},
	abstract = {About ten years ago, a small group of faculty at Worcester Poly technic Institute in Worcester, Massachusetts, took under consideration the philosophical basis upon which the traditional engineering curriculum was based, along with the methods of edu cating or training engineers. Sensing that future demands upon en gineers would be not only different from those in the past but also somewhat unpredictable in content, they sought to redefine the goals of the institution and to develop a pr?gram designed to meet the new goals. The history of their efforts, the acceptance and modification of their "Plan" by the school's entire faculty and administration, the aid provided by the National Science Foundation,  the National Endowment for the Humanities, the Carnegie  Corporation, and the Sloan Foundation, and the assessment of  results have been discussed elsewhere. My purpose is to describe the goals which the faculty set for its new program and the new (especially to engineering education) teaching and implementation strategies developed to attain those goals, and then to look at the  tentative results.},
