	abstract = {Japanese officials declared that China's prior diplomatic maneuvers had amounted to "economic warfare." In addition to its actions concerning Japan, China also halted shipments of raw rare earths to the US for ten days in October 2010, after the Obama administration opened an investigation into whether China was violating international free trade rules with its rare earth export policies. China's use of this market position as a political and diplomatic weapon aroused awareness of a problem that all but the most careful observers were unaware: The US, and the rest of the world, for that matter, is completely dependent on China for the supply of rare earth elements. To combat this rare earths crisis, Congress should mandate that all requests for proposals for defense contracts include a provision requiring contractors to guarantee supplies of weapons systems that depend on rare earth elements. Contractors who fail to supply weapons and weapons systems due to foreseeable rare earth supply problems should be subjected to liability under the False Claims Act.},
